@page "/"

<SeoHead Title="Nucleics — 100 MWh per person"
         Description="Advocacy for nuclear abundance: 100 MWh of electricity per person per year. Ending energy poverty with fission that is safe, methodical, and powerful."
         JsonLdData='new object[] { JsonLd.WebSite("Nucleics", "https://nucleics.org/") }' />

<section class="hero">
  <p class="eyebrow">Abundance through fission</p>
  <h1>Every person deserves enough electricity to thrive.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Nucleics is advocacy for a simple north star:
    <strong>100&nbsp;MWh of electricity per person per year</strong>.
    Global energy poverty is a solvable engineering problem.
    Nuclear fission is the quiet path that scales.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="triad" aria-label="Paths">
  <article id="learn" class="card">
    <h2>Learn</h2>
    <p>
      How reactors work, what the numbers mean, and why denser energy
      means less land, less fuel, and more human freedom.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article id="argue" class="card">
    <h2>Argue</h2>
    <p>
      Clear claims, careful evidence, no boom rhetoric.
      Fission is safe, methodical, reliable — boring in the best way.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article id="build" class="card">
    <h2>Build</h2>
    <p>
      Policy, plants, and people who ship.
      Abundance is built by engineers and operators, not slogans.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

<section class="metric" aria-labelledby="metric-title">
  <p class="metric-label" id="metric-title">The metric</p>
  <p class="metric-value">100 MWh<span>/person/year</span></p>
  <p class="metric-note">
    Electricity only — not total primary energy.
    Enough to lift households out of energy poverty and power
    industry, transport, desalination, and cooling as the climate warms.
    Most of the world is still far below what a modern life quietly assumes.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="why" aria-labelledby="why-title">
  <h2 id="why-title">Why nuclear</h2>
  <ul class="why-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Energy density.</strong>
      Tiny fuel mass, enormous output — the opposite of sprawling intermittency.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reliability.</strong>
      Firm, continuous power. Mission Control, not weather roulette.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Land and materials.</strong>
      High output per hectare and per tonne of steel and concrete over a plant’s life.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Scale.</strong>
      The only low-carbon source already proven at national-grid scale for decades.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="aside">
    The name nods to Asimov. The work is for people who still lack light,
    heat, and productive power — and for everyone who will need more of all three.
  </p>
</section>
